# Vascular Pathology (Gardner) — Quiz V1, 18 items (6 with images). NBME/NBOME item-writer spec; angles differ from V2.
Q = [
dict(img=None,
stem="A 63-year-old man comes to the office because of a 3-week history of chest pressure when he climbs one flight of stairs; it resolves within 5 minutes of rest. Seven months ago he underwent balloon angioplasty and placement of a bare-metal stent in the right coronary artery for the same symptom, with complete relief. He takes aspirin, clopidogrel, and atorvastatin daily as directed. His serum LDL (low-density lipoprotein)-cholesterol concentration is 62 mg/dL. Coronary angiography shows a smooth, concentric 80% narrowing confined to the stented segment, with no new plaques elsewhere. Intravascular ultrasonography shows uniform tissue lining the inside of the stent, with no lipid core and no thrombus.{FIG}Which of the following is the most likely mechanism of the recurrent narrowing?",
choices=["Intimal smooth muscle hyperplasia with matrix deposition","Formation of a new lipid-rich atheroma with a thin cap","Occlusive thrombus after stopping antiplatelet drugs","Calcium deposition within the media beneath the stent struts","Recurrent coronary vasospasm of the stented arterial segment"], key=0,
exp="Dr. Gardner's <b>stereotyped response to vascular injury</b>: a balloon strips the endothelium and stretches the media → platelets stick to exposed collagen and release growth factors → <b>smooth muscle cells (SMCs) migrate from the media into the intima, proliferate and lay down matrix</b> → <b>intimal thickening</b>, which tends toward stenosis. After stents, balloons, grafts and transplants this is called <b>fibromuscular intimal hyperplasia</b>, a common cause of graft and stent failure. <i>Board add-on:</i> drug-eluting stents (sirolimus, paclitaxel) block this SMC proliferation.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> In-stent restenosis months after angioplasty is intimal hyperplasia from smooth muscle migration and proliferation, the stereotyped response to vascular injury.",
wrong={1:"Would be correct with a lipid core on imaging and a new lesion, usually in a patient with poorly controlled lipids.",
2:"Would be correct for an abrupt acute coronary syndrome after stopping clopidogrel. He takes it, and imaging shows no thrombus.",
3:"Would be correct for Mönckeberg medial sclerosis, which does not narrow the lumen.",
4:"Would be correct for transient rest pain with ST elevation that responds to nitrates, not a fixed smooth narrowing."},
eli5="The balloon scraped the inside of the artery. Muscle cells crept in to heal the scrape, but they overdid it and grew a thick lining that narrowed the pipe again."),

dict(img=None,
stem="An investigator grows human endothelial cells on plates in a flow chamber. One set of plates is exposed to steady laminar flow. A second set is exposed to disturbed, turbulent flow with local high-flow eddies, similar to conditions at arterial branch points. After 48 hours, the cells are examined for their alignment, their rate of turnover and apoptosis, and their surface expression of vascular cell adhesion molecule-1 (VCAM-1).\nCell alignment | Turnover and apoptosis | VCAM-1 expression{FIG}Compared with the cells under laminar flow, which of the following sets of findings is most likely in the cells exposed to turbulent flow?",
choices=["Aligned with flow, decreased, decreased","Aligned with flow, increased, increased","Disorganized, decreased, decreased","Disorganized, increased, decreased","Disorganized, increased, increased"], key=4,
exp="The classic culture experiment from the lecture: under <b>laminar flow</b> endothelial cells line up like paving stones and stay in the quiet (basal) state. Under <b>turbulent flow</b> they become stressed and <b>disorganized</b>, <b>turn over faster with some apoptosis</b>, develop a rough surface, and switch to the <b>activated</b> state, expressing adhesion molecules such as <b>VCAM-1</b> that recruit monocytes. That is why <b>plaques form at branch points and ostia</b>. <i>Board add-on:</i> laminar shear induces protective genes, including endothelial nitric oxide synthase.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Turbulent flow activates endothelium (disorganized, high turnover, adhesion molecules up), which is why atherosclerosis favors branch points.",
wrong={0:"This is the laminar-flow pattern: quiet, aligned cells with low turnover and low adhesion molecules.",
1:"Alignment with flow is the laminar-flow feature. Turbulent flow scrambles the cells.",
2:"Would be correct for disorganized cells that were otherwise quiet. Turbulence raises turnover and activation.",
3:"Activated, stressed endothelium raises adhesion molecules. Decreased VCAM-1 would fit protective laminar shear."},
eli5="Smooth flow keeps the lining cells calm and lined up. Swirling flow at branch points stresses them, so they wear out faster and put out sticky molecules that catch the white cells that start plaques."),

dict(img=None,
stem="A 74-year-old man with a 60-pack-year history of cigarette smoking, hypertension, and hyperlipidemia dies of pneumonia. At autopsy, raised yellow-white plaques, many of them ulcerated and calcified, are present in multiple arteries. Microscopic sections confirm atherosclerosis in each vessel examined.{FIG}Which of the following arteries is most likely to show the most extensive involvement?",
choices=["Abdominal aorta","Circle of Willis","Coronary arteries","Internal carotid arteries","Popliteal arteries","Thoracic aorta"], key=0,
exp="Plaques are <b>patchy, not circumferential</b>, and favor branch points with turbulent flow. Dr. Gardner's order of commonest sites: <b>1 · abdominal aorta and iliacs</b> → 2 · coronary arteries → 3 · popliteal arteries → 4 · internal carotids → 5 · circle of Willis. The many branch points of the abdominal aorta, and its role as the source of most aortic aneurysms, put it first. The thoracic aorta is much less involved than the abdominal aorta.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Atherosclerosis is most extensive in the abdominal aorta and iliac arteries, followed by the coronary, popliteal, internal carotid and circle of Willis arteries.",
wrong={1:"Fifth on the lecture's list. Least involved of the named sites.",
2:"Second on the list, and clinically the most lethal, but not the most extensively involved.",
3:"Fourth on the list, at the carotid bifurcation.",
4:"Third on the list.",
5:"Involved less than the abdominal aorta, which has more branch points and more turbulent flow."},
eli5="The lower aorta in the belly gets the worst plaque because it has lots of branches where blood swirls. The heart arteries come next."),

dict(img=None,
stem="A 58-year-old man with a known glioblastoma of the left temporal lobe is evaluated in the hospital because he has become increasingly drowsy over the past 6 hours. Two days ago his pulse was 80/min and blood pressure was 132/84 mm Hg. Now his temperature is 37.0°C (98.6°F), pulse is 46/min, respirations are 8/min and irregular, and blood pressure is 194/82 mm Hg. The left pupil is 6 mm and sluggishly reactive. His only medication is levetiracetam; he has received no β-adrenergic blocking agents. An ECG shows sinus bradycardia with normal PR and QRS intervals. Serum electrolyte and thyroid-stimulating hormone concentrations are within the reference ranges.{FIG}Which of the following is the most likely mechanism of the change in this patient's heart rate?",
choices=["Baroreflex vagal response to centrally driven hypertension","Ischemic dysfunction of sinoatrial node pacemaker cells","Second-degree conduction block at the atrioventricular node","Sodium channel blockade by the antiseizure medication","Slowed metabolic rate from hypothalamic thermoregulatory injury"], key=0,
exp="A rising <b>systolic</b> pressure with a normal diastolic pressure (widened pulse pressure) plus <b>bradycardia</b> → think of an <b>intracranial</b> cause. Dr. Gardner's pearl: the <b>Cushing reflex</b> of raised intracranial pressure. The expanding tumor raises intracranial pressure → cerebral perfusion falls → a sympathetic surge raises systemic pressure to perfuse the brain → carotid and aortic <b>baroreceptors</b> fire → vagal output → <b>bradycardia</b>. <i>Board add-on:</i> the full Cushing triad adds irregular respirations from brainstem compression, a sign of impending herniation (the dilated left pupil).<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Hypertension with a wide pulse pressure, bradycardia and irregular breathing signals raised intracranial pressure (the Cushing reflex).",
wrong={1:"Would be correct with an inferior myocardial infarction (right coronary territory) and ischemic ECG changes.",
2:"Ruled out by the normal PR interval and the 1:1 conduction on ECG.",
3:"Levetiracetam acts on a synaptic vesicle protein (SV2A). It doesn't block sodium channels or slow the heart.",
4:"Would be correct with hypothermia or hypothyroidism. His temperature and thyroid-stimulating hormone are normal."},
eli5="Swelling in his skull squeezes the brain, so his body cranks up blood pressure to push blood in. Pressure sensors in the neck then slam the brakes on the heart rate."),

dict(img=None,
stem="A 68-year-old man comes to the office because his blood pressure has risen from 132/80 mm Hg to 178/98 mm Hg over 8 months despite taking his medications as directed. He has a 50-pack-year history of cigarette smoking and has cramping in both calves after walking 2 blocks. His only medication is amlodipine; he takes no diuretics. A bruit is heard in the left upper quadrant of the abdomen. Serum creatinine concentration is 1.4 mg/dL. Renal duplex ultrasonography shows an 80% narrowing at the origin of the left renal artery, and the left kidney is 2 cm shorter than the right.\nPlasma renin activity | Serum aldosterone | Serum potassium{FIG}Which of the following sets of laboratory findings is most likely in this patient?",
choices=["Decreased, decreased, decreased","Decreased, decreased, increased","Decreased, increased, decreased","Increased, decreased, increased","Increased, increased, decreased"], key=4,
exp="An atherosclerotic narrowing at the renal artery origin → <b>low pressure at the afferent arteriole</b> → the kidney releases <b>renin</b> ↑ → renin cleaves angiotensinogen to angiotensin I → endothelial ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) makes <b>angiotensin II</b> → vasoconstriction and adrenal <b>aldosterone</b> ↑ → sodium retained, <b>potassium excreted</b> ↓. This is <b>secondary hyperaldosteronism</b>, a renal cause of secondary hypertension. Dr. Gardner's five-step RAAS (renin–angiotensin–aldosterone system).<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Renal artery stenosis raises renin, which raises angiotensin II and aldosterone and causes hypokalemia (secondary hyperaldosteronism).",
wrong={0:"Would be correct for a non-aldosterone mineralocorticoid excess, such as licorice or Cushing syndrome, which suppresses both renin and aldosterone.",
1:"Would be correct for hyporeninemic hypoaldosteronism (type 4 renal tubular acidosis), often in diabetic kidney disease.",
2:"Would be correct for primary aldosteronism (an aldosterone-secreting adrenal adenoma), where high aldosterone suppresses renin.",
3:"Would be correct for primary adrenal insufficiency (Addison disease), where the adrenal can't make aldosterone despite high renin."},
eli5="The kidney behind the blocked artery thinks blood pressure is too low, so it pumps out renin. That chain raises aldosterone, which keeps salt, raises pressure and dumps potassium."),

dict(img=None,
stem="An 81-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department because of a 5-day history of lightheadedness and episodes of confusion, and one brief fall without injury. Ten days ago her blood pressure was 186/96 mm Hg at her first office visit in many years, and she began taking amlodipine and lisinopril. Now her temperature is 37.0°C (98.6°F) and pulse is 76/min. Her blood pressure is 118/68 mm Hg supine and 110/64 mm Hg standing, with a pulse of 84/min standing. Neurologic examination shows no focal findings. Serum sodium and glucose concentrations are within the reference ranges. CT of the head shows no hemorrhage or infarct.{FIG}Which of the following best explains this patient's symptoms?",
choices=["Arterioles too stiff and narrow to dilate at lower pressure","Embolic occlusion of a branch of the middle cerebral artery","Hyponatremia from drug-induced antidiuretic hormone release","Small hemorrhage from a ruptured Charcot-Bouchard aneurysm","Autonomic failure abolishing the baroreceptor heart-rate reflex"], key=0,
exp="Dr. Gardner's <b>treatment trap</b>: chronic hypertension → endothelial injury, protein leakage and smooth muscle proliferation → <b>fixed, narrowed arterioles</b> that can no longer relax → end-organ blood flow now depends on high pressure. Dropping a long-standing pressure quickly to \"normal\" <b>under-perfuses the brain</b>, which is common when elderly patients start antihypertensives for the first time. <i>Board add-on:</i> the cerebral autoregulation curve shifts to the right in chronic hypertension, so lower blood pressure gradually.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> In long-standing hypertension, fixed arteriolar narrowing makes a rapid drop to normal pressure cause cerebral hypoperfusion; lower it gradually.",
wrong={1:"Would be correct with a sudden focal deficit (weakness, aphasia) and an infarct on imaging.",
2:"Ruled out by the normal serum sodium.",
3:"Would be correct for a hypertensive hemorrhage with focal signs and blood on CT.",
4:"Would be correct for true orthostatic hypotension, a drop of 20/10 mm Hg or more without a rise in pulse. Her reflexes are intact."},
eli5="After years of high pressure, the small brain arteries became stiff, narrow pipes. When her pressure dropped quickly, not enough blood could squeeze through, so she got dizzy and confused."),

dict(img='Vberry', cap='CT angiography of the head, 3-dimensional reconstruction viewed from above (arrow)',
stem="A 44-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department 2 hours after the sudden onset of a severe headache that began while she was lifting weights; she describes it as the worst headache of her life. She has vomited twice. She has autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease and hypertension. Her temperature is 37.1°C (98.8°F), pulse is 92/min, and blood pressure is 168/98 mm Hg. She has neck stiffness and no focal weakness. She has no history of head trauma, fever, or heart murmur. Noncontrast CT of the head shows blood in the basal cisterns. A CT angiogram is shown.{FIG}Which of the following best describes the pathogenesis of the vascular lesion indicated by the arrow?",
choices=["Congenital medial weakness at an arterial branch","Atherosclerotic plaque eroding through the wall","Infection of the wall seeded by a septic embolus","Hypertensive damage to small penetrating arterioles","Granulomatous inflammation destroying the elastic lamina","Traumatic tear contained by surrounding tissue"], key=0,
exp="A saccular outpouching at a <b>branch point of the circle of Willis</b> (here the middle cerebral artery) with blood in the subarachnoid space = a ruptured <b>berry aneurysm</b> → <b>subarachnoid hemorrhage</b> (\"worst headache of my life\"). Dr. Gardner: berry aneurysms are <b>primarily congenital</b>, a weakness of the media where arteries branch. <i>Board add-on:</i> associated with autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease, Ehlers–Danlos syndrome and coarctation; hypertension and smoking raise the rupture risk; the commonest site is the anterior communicating artery.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Berry (saccular) aneurysms arise from congenital medial weakness at circle of Willis branch points and rupture to cause subarachnoid hemorrhage.",
wrong={1:"Atherosclerosis drives abdominal aortic and peripheral aneurysms, not berry aneurysms.",
2:"Would be correct for a mycotic aneurysm, with fever and endocarditis (a murmur, positive blood cultures).",
3:"Would be correct for Charcot-Bouchard microaneurysms causing deep intraparenchymal (basal ganglia) hemorrhage.",
4:"Would be correct for giant cell arteritis, which involves extracranial branches of the carotid in older patients.",
5:"Would be correct for a false aneurysm (pseudoaneurysm) after head trauma."},
eli5="She was born with a weak spot where two brain arteries split. Over the years it bulged into a berry, and straining popped it, bleeding around the brain."),

dict(img='Vascan', cap='Contrast-enhanced CT of the chest, axial view',
stem="A 64-year-old man comes to the office because of a 6-month history of shortness of breath with exertion. Thirty years ago he had a painless genital ulcer that healed without treatment. His pulse is 82/min, and blood pressure is 154/52 mm Hg. He is 175 cm (5 ft 9 in) tall with normal body proportions, and his lenses are in normal position. An early diastolic decrescendo murmur is heard at the right upper sternal border. Rapid plasma reagin titer is 1:8, and the fluorescent treponemal antibody absorption test is positive. Erythrocyte sedimentation rate is 14 mm/h. Blood cultures are negative. CT of the chest is shown; the ascending aorta measures 6.8 cm, and the descending and abdominal aorta are normal in diameter, without calcified plaques or an intimal flap.{FIG}Which of the following is the most likely mechanism of the aortic dilation?",
choices=["Endarteritis of the vasa vasorum starving the media","Fibrillin-1 deficiency causing medial degeneration","Atherosclerotic plaque weakening the aortic wall","Granulomatous giant cell inflammation of the aortic wall","Bacterial infection of the wall from bacteremia","Intimal tear with blood tracking through the media"], key=0,
exp="An aneurysm of the <b>ascending (thoracic) aorta</b>, decades after untreated syphilis (positive treponemal test) = <b>tertiary syphilitic aortitis</b>. Dr. Gardner: syphilis inflames and <b>blocks the vasa vasorum</b> (the wall's own blood supply in thick vessels) → the media becomes ischemic and dies back → the wall weakens and dilates. Root dilation stretches the valve ring → <b>aortic regurgitation</b> (wide pulse pressure, early diastolic murmur). <i>Board add-on:</i> the intima looks like \"tree bark.\"<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Tertiary syphilis causes ascending aortic aneurysm by obliterative endarteritis of the vasa vasorum, often with aortic regurgitation.",
wrong={1:"Would be correct for Marfan syndrome: tall, long limbs, lens dislocation, usually younger.",
2:"Would be correct for an infrarenal abdominal aortic aneurysm with calcified plaque.",
3:"Would be correct for giant cell aortitis, with headache, jaw claudication and a raised ESR.",
4:"Would be correct for a mycotic aneurysm, with fever and positive blood cultures.",
5:"Would be correct for aortic dissection, with tearing pain and an intimal flap on CT."},
eli5="Old untreated syphilis choked the tiny vessels that feed the aorta's wall. The starved wall weakened and ballooned, stretching the aortic valve so it leaks."),

dict(img=None,
stem="A 19-year-old man is evaluated before joining his college basketball team. He is 198 cm (6 ft 6 in) tall, and his arm span is 208 cm. He has long, thin fingers, a sunken sternum, and upward displacement of both lenses. A midsystolic click is heard at the apex. His skin is not hyperextensible, and he has no history of easy bruising. Echocardiography shows dilation of the aortic root to 4.6 cm. His father died suddenly at age 38 of a tear in the ascending aorta.{FIG}The defective protein in this patient normally helps restrain the activity of which of the following signaling molecules in the aortic wall?",
choices=["Endothelin-1","Nitric oxide","Platelet-derived growth factor","Transforming growth factor-β","Vascular endothelial growth factor"], key=3,
exp="Tall with long limbs, upward lens dislocation, mitral valve prolapse (midsystolic click) and aortic root dilation = <b>Marfan syndrome</b>, a defect in <b>fibrillin-1</b>. Fibrillin microfibrils scaffold elastic fibers and normally hold <b>latent TGF-β (transforming growth factor-β)</b> in the matrix. The lecture lists \"altered TGF-β signalling\" among causes of weak vessel walls: defective fibrillin → excess TGF-β activity → medial degeneration → root dilation, <b>type A dissection</b> (lifetime risk up to 30%) and aortic regurgitation. Mitral valve prolapse occurs in more than 90%.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Marfan syndrome (fibrillin-1 defect) weakens the aortic media partly through excess TGF-β signaling, causing root dilation and type A dissection.",
wrong={0:"Endothelin-1 is an endothelial vasoconstrictor. It is not held by fibrillin microfibrils.",
1:"Nitric oxide is the endothelial vasodilator. Its loss is part of endothelial dysfunction, not Marfan syndrome.",
2:"Would be correct for the factor that recruits smooth muscle cells into the intima in atherosclerosis and restenosis.",
4:"Would be correct for the angiogenic factor driving new vessel growth, as in tumors and diabetic retinopathy."},
eli5="Fibrillin normally holds a growth signal locked up in the artery wall. When fibrillin is faulty, too much signal gets loose and the aorta wall weakens and stretches."),

dict(img=None,
stem="A 70-year-old man has a tender, pulsatile mass in the right groin 2 days after cardiac catheterization performed through the right femoral artery. His pulse is 84/min, and blood pressure is 138/82 mm Hg. A bruit is heard over the mass. Duplex ultrasonography shows a 3-cm collection with swirling flow, connected to the front wall of the femoral artery by a narrow channel. The femoral artery itself is normal in diameter, and there is no intimal flap. His temperature is 37.0°C (98.6°F), and his leukocyte count is normal.{FIG}Which of the following best describes the wall of this lesion?",
choices=["Hematoma contained by perivascular connective tissue","All three arterial layers bulging on one side of the wall","All three layers dilated around the full circumference","Blood tracking within the media through an intimal tear","Arterial wall destroyed by bacterial infection"], key=0,
exp="A pulsatile collection joined to the artery by a narrow neck after a <b>puncture</b> = a <b>false aneurysm (pseudoaneurysm)</b>: the wall is breached (trauma, a catheter, local atherosclerosis) → blood forms an <b>extravascular hematoma</b> held in by the adventitia and surrounding tissue. The vessel layers themselves have <b>not</b> dilated. Dr. Gardner's vocabulary: true aneurysms are <b>saccular</b> (one side, all layers) or <b>fusiform</b> (circumferential); a <b>dissection</b> splits the media.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> A pseudoaneurysm is a contained extravascular hematoma communicating with the lumen, not a dilation of the arterial layers.",
wrong={1:"Would be correct for a true saccular aneurysm, such as a berry aneurysm.",
2:"Would be correct for a true fusiform aneurysm, such as most abdominal aortic aneurysms. The femoral artery here is normal in diameter.",
3:"Would be correct for a dissection, with an intimal flap and a false lumen.",
4:"Would be correct for a mycotic aneurysm, with fever and a raised leukocyte count."},
eli5="The catheter left a hole in the artery. Blood leaked out and is held in a pocket by the tissue around it, not by the artery wall itself."),

dict(img='Vgcahisto', cap='Photomicrograph of a temporal artery biopsy specimen (H&E; arrow)',
stem="A 77-year-old woman comes to the office because of a 2-week history of headache, scalp tenderness when she brushes her hair, and pain in the jaw when chewing. For 3 months she has had aching and stiffness of the shoulders and hips each morning. She has no cough, rash, or nasal symptoms, and she has had no fever above 38°C. Her blood pressure is 136/80 mm Hg. Erythrocyte sedimentation rate is 88 mm/h. Urinalysis shows no abnormalities. A photomicrograph of a temporal artery biopsy specimen is shown.{FIG}Without treatment, this patient is at greatest immediate risk for which of the following?",
choices=["Coronary artery aneurysm","Gangrene of the fingertips","Irreversible loss of vision","Mononeuritis multiplex","Pulmonary hemorrhage","Rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis"], key=2,
exp="Granulomatous inflammation of the artery wall with a <b>multinucleated giant cell</b> (arrow), thickened intima and a fragmented internal elastic lamina = <b>giant cell (temporal) arteritis</b>, the large-vessel vasculitis of people over 50, associated with <b>polymyalgia rheumatica</b> (shoulder and hip stiffness). Its feared complication is <b>ophthalmic artery involvement → blindness</b> if untreated, so steroids start immediately. Dr. Gardner: relatively common for a vasculitis, and very treatable. <i>Board add-on:</i> years later, thoracic aortic aneurysm.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Giant cell arteritis threatens irreversible vision loss through ophthalmic artery involvement.",
wrong={0:"Would be correct for Kawasaki disease in a child younger than 5 years.",
1:"Would be correct for thromboangiitis obliterans (Buerger disease) in a heavy smoker.",
3:"Would be correct for polyarteritis nodosa or eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis.",
4:"Would be correct for microscopic polyangiitis, granulomatosis with polyangiitis or anti-GBM disease.",
5:"Would be correct for the small-vessel ANCA vasculitides. Giant cell arteritis spares the kidney, and her urinalysis is normal."},
eli5="The inflamed temple artery is a branch of the same system that feeds the eye. If the eye artery clogs, sight can be lost for good, which is why treatment starts right away."),

dict(img=None,
stem="A 3-year-old boy is brought to the emergency department because of a 6-day history of fever up to 39.8°C (103.6°F) that has not responded to acetaminophen. For 4 days his eyes have been red without discharge. He has been irritable and is eating poorly. His temperature is 39.4°C (102.9°F), pulse is 150/min, and blood pressure is 96/58 mm Hg. Examination shows bilateral conjunctival injection that spares the area around the iris, red cracked lips, a red tongue with prominent papillae, a 2-cm tender left cervical lymph node, a blotchy rash on the trunk, and swollen, red hands and feet. He has received all recommended vaccinations. There are no Koplik spots, and the neck is supple. A rapid streptococcal antigen test is negative.{FIG}Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in evaluation?",
choices=["Antistreptolysin O titer","Blood cultures","Echocardiography","Lumbar puncture","Measles IgM serology"], key=2,
exp="Fever for ≥5 days plus <b>CRASH</b> (Conjunctivitis, Rash, Adenopathy (cervical), Strawberry tongue/red cracked lips, Hands and feet swollen) = <b>Kawasaki disease</b>, a <b>medium-vessel vasculitis</b> of children under 5 that targets the <b>coronary arteries</b>. The must-know complication is <b>coronary artery aneurysm</b> → get an <b>echocardiogram</b>. <i>Board add-on:</i> treat promptly with intravenous immunoglobulin (IVIG) and aspirin.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Kawasaki disease (fever ≥5 days + CRASH) requires echocardiography to look for coronary artery aneurysms.",
wrong={0:"Would be correct for suspected acute rheumatic fever after a strep throat, with carditis, arthritis or chorea.",
1:"Would be correct for suspected bacteremia or sepsis without this mucocutaneous pattern.",
3:"Would be correct with neck stiffness, a bulging fontanelle or altered consciousness from meningitis. His neck is supple.",
4:"Would be correct for an unvaccinated child with cough, coryza, Koplik spots and a rash spreading from head down."},
eli5="This fever with red eyes, cracked lips and swollen hands is Kawasaki disease. It can make bulges in the heart's own arteries, so you look at the heart with ultrasound."),

dict(img=None,
stem="Patient Information\nAge: 34 years\nGender: male\nSite of Care: office\n\nHistory\nReason for Visit: painful sore on the tip of the left index finger\nHistory of Present Illness:\n• 3 months of cramping pain in the arches of both feet after walking 1 block\n• 6 weeks of fingers turning white, then blue, in cold weather\n• 2 weeks of a painful ulcer on the tip of the left index finger\n• 1 year ago: tender red cords along a vein of the left forearm, and later of the right calf, each resolving in about 2 weeks\nPast Medical History: none\nMedications: none\nPsychosocial History: smokes 2 packs of cigarettes daily for 16 years; drinks 2 beers on weekends; no other substance use\n\nPhysical Examination\nTemp 37.0°C (98.6°F) · Pulse 78/min · BP 124/78 mm Hg\n• Radial pulses 2+; ulnar pulses absent bilaterally\n• Femoral and popliteal pulses 2+; dorsalis pedis pulses absent bilaterally\n• 4-mm ulcer on the tip of the left index finger\n\nDiagnostic Studies\n• Fasting lipids and glucose: within reference ranges\n• Antinuclear antibody: negative\n• Echocardiography: no source of embolus\n\nQuestion:{FIG}Which of the following is the most effective intervention to prevent amputation in this patient?",
choices=["Complete cessation of tobacco use","Lifelong warfarin anticoagulation","High-dose oral glucocorticoids","High-intensity statin therapy","Distal arterial bypass surgery"], key=0,
exp="A young heavy smoker with <b>distal</b> limb ischemia in arms and legs (absent ulnar and pedal pulses with normal proximal pulses), Raynaud phenomenon, a digital ulcer and <b>superficial migratory thrombophlebitis</b> = <b>Buerger disease (thromboangiitis obliterans)</b>, a medium-vessel vasculitis of the limbs that does not affect organs and always shows thrombosis. Dr. Gardner: tobacco-driven autoimmunity, and the <b>only cure is to stop smoking</b>. Even a few cigarettes keep the disease active.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> The only effective treatment for thromboangiitis obliterans is complete tobacco cessation.",
wrong={1:"Would be correct for a cardiac embolic source or an inherited thrombophilia. Echocardiography is normal.",
2:"Would be correct for giant cell or Takayasu arteritis. Steroids don't halt tobacco-driven Buerger disease.",
3:"Would be correct for atherosclerotic peripheral artery disease. His lipids and glucose are normal.",
4:"Rarely possible here: the disease is distal and segmental with few target vessels, and it progresses if smoking continues."},
eli5="Smoking is making his immune system attack and clot the small arteries of his hands and feet. Stopping smoking completely is the only thing that saves his fingers and toes."),

dict(img=None,
stem="A 45-year-old man comes to the office because of a 3-month history of nasal stuffiness with bloody, crusted discharge and a 3-week history of cough with blood-streaked sputum. He has no history of asthma or allergies. His temperature is 38.0°C (100.4°F), pulse is 92/min, and blood pressure is 148/92 mm Hg. The nasal mucosa is ulcerated, and the septum is perforated. CT of the chest shows three nodules, two of which have cavitated. Urinalysis shows red blood cell casts. The eosinophil count is normal. Anti–glomerular basement membrane antibody test is negative. A tuberculin skin test shows 2 mm of induration.{FIG}A biopsy specimen of one lung nodule is most likely to show which of the following?",
choices=["Necrotizing granulomatous inflammation with vasculitis","Necrotizing small-vessel vasculitis without granulomas","Eosinophil-rich granulomas around small vessels","Linear IgG deposits along alveolar basement membranes","Caseating granulomas containing acid-fast bacilli","Tight noncaseating granulomas without vasculitis"], key=0,
exp="Upper airway (bloody crusted sinusitis, septal perforation) + lung (cavitating nodules) + kidney (red blood cell casts) = <b>granulomatosis with polyangiitis (GPA, formerly Wegener)</b>. Histology: <b>necrotizing granulomas with small-vessel vasculitis</b> in airway, lung and kidney. Serology: <b>c-ANCA against proteinase 3 (PR3)</b> in about 75%. Dr. Gardner's review table marks granulomas as \"always\" present in GPA.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Granulomatosis with polyangiitis shows necrotizing granulomatous inflammation with small-vessel vasculitis of the upper airway, lung and kidney (c-ANCA, anti-PR3).",
wrong={1:"Would be correct for microscopic polyangiitis: kidney and lung without sinus disease or nodules, p-ANCA (anti-MPO).",
2:"Would be correct for eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis, with asthma and eosinophilia.",
3:"Would be correct for anti-GBM disease. The antibody test is negative.",
4:"Would be correct for tuberculosis. The skin test is negative, and TB doesn't cause red cell casts.",
5:"Would be correct for sarcoidosis, with hilar adenopathy and no vasculitis or red cell casts."},
eli5="This disease attacks the small vessels of the nose, lungs and kidneys and forms clusters of inflamed cells (granulomas) that eat holes in tissue."),

dict(img='Vegpa', cap='Photomicrograph of a biopsy specimen of an involved small artery (H&E)',
stem="A 41-year-old woman comes to the office because of a 2-month history of numbness and weakness of the left foot, followed 3 weeks later by weakness of the right wrist. She developed asthma 4 years ago, at age 37, and has needed increasing doses of inhaled glucocorticoids; she also has nasal polyps. Her temperature is 37.8°C (100.0°F), pulse is 88/min, and blood pressure is 128/80 mm Hg. Scattered expiratory wheezes are heard. She has a left foot drop, a right wrist drop, and palpable purpura on both legs. Leukocyte count is 13,800/mm³ with 22% eosinophils. Serum creatinine concentration is 0.9 mg/dL. Stool examination for ova and parasites is negative. Hepatitis B surface antigen is negative. A biopsy specimen of an involved small artery is shown.{FIG}Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
choices=["Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis","Eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis","Granulomatosis with polyangiitis","Microscopic polyangiitis","Polyarteritis nodosa"], key=1,
exp="Adult-onset <b>asthma</b>, nasal polyps (atopy), marked <b>eosinophilia</b>, <b>mononeuritis multiplex</b> (foot drop, then wrist drop) and purpura, with a necrotizing vasculitis whose wall and surroundings are packed with <b>eosinophils</b> = <b>eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis (EGPA, formerly Churg–Strauss)</b>. ANCA: p-ANCA (anti-MPO) in about 45%; about half are ANCA-negative. It sometimes involves the kidney and heart. Dr. Gardner's table: eosinophils \"always.\"<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Asthma + eosinophilia + mononeuritis multiplex with an eosinophil-rich small-vessel vasculitis is eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis.",
wrong={0:"Would be correct for worsening asthma with eosinophilia, central bronchiectasis and high IgE, but without vasculitis, neuropathy or purpura.",
2:"Would be correct with sinus destruction, cavitating lung nodules and red cell casts, without asthma or eosinophilia.",
3:"Would be correct for kidney and lung capillaritis without asthma or eosinophilia.",
4:"Would be correct for mononeuritis multiplex with hepatitis B and visceral microaneurysms, but no asthma and the lungs spared."},
eli5="Her asthma came with a flood of allergy-type white cells (eosinophils) that went on to attack small arteries feeding her nerves and skin, causing foot and wrist drop."),

dict(img=None,
stem="A 29-year-old man comes to the office because of a 2-year history of recurrent painful mouth ulcers, 3 to 4 times yearly, each lasting 1 to 2 weeks. He has also had two episodes of painful scrotal ulcers that healed with scarring. Over the past week, his right eye has become red and painful with blurred vision. He grew up in Turkey and moved to the United States 3 years ago. His temperature is 37.6°C (99.7°F). There are three 6-mm shallow ulcers with a yellow base on the buccal mucosa. The right eye shows a layer of pus in the anterior chamber. There are tender red nodules on both shins. A pustule formed at a venipuncture site 48 hours after blood was drawn. Polymerase chain reaction testing of an oral ulcer for herpes simplex virus is negative, and a rapid plasma reagin test is negative.{FIG}This patient's condition is most strongly associated with which of the following HLA alleles?",
choices=["HLA-B27","HLA-B51","HLA-DQ2","HLA-DR3","HLA-DR4"], key=1,
exp="Recurrent <b>oral and genital ulcers</b> + <b>uveitis</b> (hypopyon) + skin lesions (erythema nodosum, a pustule at a needle stick) = <b>Behçet disease</b>, a <b>variable-vessel vasculitis</b> that hits arteries and veins of any size. Dr. Gardner: rare in the US (about 5 per 100,000) but very common in the Middle East and Turkey (about 500 per 100,000), linked to <b>HLA-B51</b>. Aneurysms and renal failure are its biggest consequences; neutrophils are always present. <i>Board add-on:</i> the needle-stick pustule is the pathergy test.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Behçet disease (orogenital ulcers + uveitis, variable-vessel vasculitis) is associated with HLA-B51.",
wrong={0:"Would be correct for ankylosing spondylitis or reactive arthritis, with back pain or arthritis and anterior uveitis but no orogenital ulcers.",
2:"Would be correct for celiac disease.",
3:"Would be correct for type 1 diabetes, Graves disease or systemic lupus erythematosus.",
4:"Would be correct for rheumatoid arthritis."},
eli5="This disease inflames blood vessels of every size and causes painful mouth and genital sores and eye inflammation. It is tied to a gene type, HLA-B51, that's common along the old Silk Road."),

dict(img='Vsaddle', cap='Gross photograph at autopsy',
stem="A 67-year-old woman collapses and dies suddenly 6 days after a total right hip replacement. She had been reluctant to walk because of pain, and her pharmacologic thromboprophylaxis was stopped on postoperative day 2 because of wound oozing. On the morning of her death, her right thigh and calf were noted to be swollen. She had no history of irregular heart rhythm or myocardial infarction. At autopsy, the finding shown is present at the bifurcation of the main pulmonary artery; microscopic examination shows lines of Zahn and no fat globules.{FIG}This material most likely originated in which of the following locations?",
choices=["Deep veins of the thigh and pelvis","Left atrial appendage","Marrow fat released during surgery","Portal venous system","Right ventricular wall over an infarct","Superficial varicose veins of the calf"], key=0,
exp="A long, coiled thrombus straddling the pulmonary artery bifurcation = a <b>saddle embolus</b>, which blocks both sides at once → rapid death. Dr. Gardner: a <b>long leg-vein thrombus</b> travels through the right heart and lodges there. Thrombophlebitis needs <b>stasis</b> (immobility after surgery) + <b>hypercoagulability</b> (surgery, stopped prophylaxis). The swollen thigh and calf point to an iliofemoral deep vein thrombosis. Lines of Zahn show it formed in flowing blood before it embolized.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Saddle pulmonary emboli come from large deep vein thromboses of the thigh and pelvis formed through stasis and hypercoagulability.",
wrong={1:"Would be correct for a systemic (arterial) embolus to the brain or limbs in atrial fibrillation. It can't reach the pulmonary artery.",
2:"Would be correct for fat embolism 1–3 days after a long-bone fracture or orthopedic surgery, with fat globules in small vessels.",
3:"Portal venous blood drains into the liver sinusoids, which trap clots. It doesn't send emboli to the lungs.",
4:"Would be correct for a mural thrombus over an infarct, but she had no myocardial infarction.",
5:"Superficial varicose veins cause stasis, edema and ulcers but rarely embolize."},
eli5="A big clot formed in her leg vein while she lay still after surgery. It broke off, traveled through the heart and jammed where the lung artery splits, blocking both lungs at once."),

dict(img='Vangio', cap='Photomicrographs of a liver mass: H&E (left) and immunohistochemical stain for CD31, an endothelial marker (right)',
stem="A 62-year-old man comes to the office because of a 2-month history of right upper quadrant pain, early satiety, and a 6-kg (13-lb) weight loss. For 28 years he worked in a plant that polymerized a gas into the resin used for plastic pipes, without respiratory protection. He does not drink alcohol. His temperature is 37.2°C (99.0°F). The liver is palpable 5 cm below the costal margin. Tests for hepatitis B and C are negative, and serum α-fetoprotein concentration is within the reference range. CT shows multiple hemorrhagic masses in the liver. Photomicrographs of a biopsy specimen are shown.{FIG}This tumor is most likely related to which of the following exposures?",
choices=["Aflatoxin B1","Aniline dyes","Asbestos","Benzene","Radon","Vinyl chloride"], key=5,
exp="Irregular, atypical vascular channels lined by malignant cells that stain for <b>CD31</b> (an endothelial marker), forming hemorrhagic liver masses = <b>hepatic angiosarcoma</b>. Dr. Gardner: vanishingly rare, and the classic occupational cause is <b>vinyl chloride</b>, the gas polymerized into PVC (polyvinyl chloride) plastic, now mostly historical. Angiosarcoma is locally aggressive and metastasizes; Kaposi sarcoma is the more indolent malignant vascular tumor.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Hepatic angiosarcoma (CD31-positive malignant vascular tumor) is linked to vinyl chloride exposure in PVC manufacturing.",
wrong={0:"Would be correct for hepatocellular carcinoma, usually with a raised α-fetoprotein and moldy grain or peanut exposure.",
1:"Would be correct for urothelial (bladder) carcinoma in dye and rubber workers.",
2:"Would be correct for mesothelioma or lung carcinoma in shipyard, insulation or brake workers.",
3:"Would be correct for acute myeloid leukemia and aplastic anemia in petrochemical or shoe-glue workers.",
4:"Would be correct for lung carcinoma in miners or from household radon."},
eli5="Breathing a chemical used to make plastic pipe for decades turned cells lining his liver's blood vessels into a cancer that forms bloody tumors."),
]
